<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Robots on Earth and in Space</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Future Exploration of Space</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Conduct a presentation on a general skill. Interactive: Participate in debates on abstract concepts.
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can conduct a presentation demonstrating a general skill, such as using a new app (e.g., "I will demonstrate how to operate a robotic arm used in Panama's agricultural sector to improve planting efficiency.")
      Can participate in debates on abstract concepts related to technology (e.g., "Robot technology might replace some human roles, but it's unlikely to fully take over creative or emotional jobs.")
      Can respond to questions after a presentation, clarifying details of the skill (e.g., "How does this satellite navigation system work?" "This system tracks the spacecraft's position by communicating with multiple satellites and calculating precise coordinates.")
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various robots (e.g., a *robot* in an *agriculture* field, a *robot* arm in a *factory*, a Mars *rover* used for *space exploration*). Students are prompted to discuss in pairs: "Which of these *robots* do you find most *useful* or interesting? Why? How do you think *technology* will change the way we *explore* *space* in the future?" Students share their initial thoughts with the class, activating prior knowledge and introducing the theme of *The Future Exploration of Space*. The teacher encourages the use of words like *operate*, *control*, *efficiency*, *research*, and *mission* from the previous lessons.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express opinions and predictions about *robots* and *space exploration* using target grammar. For example, "By 2040, *robots* *will have launched* many new *satellites* into *orbit*." (Future perfect) or "If *robots* are *programmed* correctly, they *perform* tasks with *precision* and *safety*." (Zero conditional). The teacher specifically emphasizes the pronunciation of /r/ in words like "*robots*", "*research*", "*repair*", and /s/ in words like "*space*", "*sensors*", "*satellite*". The teacher also models the pronunciation of -ed endings, e.g., "controlled" (/d/), "launched" (/t/), "operated" (/ɪd/).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of concepts and pronunciation.
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "When I say 'By 2050, *robots* *will have discovered* new *planets*', am I talking about something that happened in the past, or something that will be completed in the future?" (Expected answer: "Something completed in the future.")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "When I say '*operated*', do you hear a /d/, /t/, or /ɪd/ sound at the end?" (Expected answer: "/ɪd/"). The teacher guides students to repeat the word.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (2-3 minute) audio clip or video excerpt from a science documentary or an expert interview discussing the future of *robotics* in *space exploration* and on Earth (e.g., *automated* *agriculture* or *medicine*). The content should present both the advantages and potential challenges of *robot* *technology*. It includes key vocabulary: *automation*, *industry*, *sensor*, *repair*, *mission*, *telescope*, *research*, *gravity*, *unexplored*, and explicitly uses passive voice and future perfect structures.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding and interpreting information about scientific and technological advancements in a general context to prepare for discussion.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen/watch the input.
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Oral):</b> "What is the main idea of this clip? What are the biggest benefits mentioned for *robots* in *space*? What are the main concerns?" (Students discuss answers in pairs, then share briefly with the class, focusing on capturing the main points orally.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Oral):</b> The teacher provides a few statements (e.g., "By 2035, new types of *robots* will have *repaired* *space stations*." "Data about *distant* *planets* *is collected* by *automated* *telescopes*."). Students orally identify if these statements were mentioned in the clip and explain why or why not, using their own words.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing accurate use of future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional, and target vocabulary related to *robots* and *space exploration* in spoken sentences and short phrases. Focus on pronunciation of /r/, /s/, and -ed endings.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Oral Production):</b> Students are given sentence stems on slips of paper and complete them orally with a partner, ensuring correct grammar and vocabulary. Examples: "By the next decade, *robotics* __________ (will have transformed / industries)." "Much *research* __________ (is collected / by *sensors*)." "If *machines* are *programmed* well, they __________ (*operate* / *efficiently*)." Students read their completed sentences aloud, paying attention to the pronunciation of /r/, /s/ and -ed endings.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Scramble & Rephrasing):</b> In small groups, students receive sets of jumbled words to form sentences related to the theme, then rephrase them using a different target verb or noun while maintaining the grammar structure. Example: "galaxy / will have explored / by 2070 / robots / a distant". (Original: "By 2070, *robots* *will have explored* a *distant* *galaxy*.") Rephrasing idea: "By 2070, new *equipment* *will have launched* to *discover* *unexplored* *planets*." This encourages flexibility and expanding vocabulary.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Question & Answer Chain):</b> Students form pairs. One student asks a question using the zero conditional or passive voice about *robots* in *industry* or *space*. The other student answers using the future perfect, incorporating at least two target vocabulary words. Then roles switch. Example: Student A: "If *data is collected* by *robots* in *agriculture*, what *will have improved* by next year?" Student B: "If *data is collected* by *robots*, crop *efficiency* *will have improved*, and manual *labor* *will have been automated*." The teacher circulates, providing corrective feedback on grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation, especially on -ed endings, /r/, and /s/.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space")</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Debate / Panel Discussion. Students are divided into groups of 4-5. Each group selects a role:
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group A: Space Exploration Advocates (e.g., NASA scientists, private *space* company representatives)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group B: Earth Industry Innovators (e.g., *robotics* engineers in *agriculture* or *medicine*)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group C: Societal Impact Ethicists (e.g., philosophers, public policy experts)</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each group prepares a short (2-3 minute) opening statement presenting their perspective on the prompt: "The inevitable rise of *robots* in *space exploration* and terrestrial *industries*: benefits, challenges, and ethical considerations." Students must incorporate the target grammar (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional) and at least 8-10 target vocabulary words (e.g., *automation*, *research*, *safety*, *control*, *mission*, *planet*, *exploration*, *equipment*, *repair*). They should also consider the pragmatic function of encouraging respectful dialogue and offering solutions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After opening statements, a moderated debate/discussion ensues (2-3 rounds of exchanges), where groups respond to each other's points and questions. Students are encouraged to participate actively in debates on abstract concepts related to *technology* and to clarify details of their arguments. For example, a student from Group B might demonstrate how to *operate* a *robotic arm* (verbally explaining the process) to improve *planting efficiency* as part of their argument for *automation* in *agriculture*, addressing the learning outcome "conduct a presentation demonstrating a general skill".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher acts as a facilitator, ensuring all viewpoints are considered, and students practice the sociolinguistic function of offering solutions while maintaining respectful dialogue.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on active participation, appropriate use of target grammar and vocabulary, clarity of arguments, and pronunciation. Peer assessment during the debate (each group evaluates another group's use of target language and effectiveness).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group debate/panel discussion, delivering their arguments, responding to questions, and clarifying details, demonstrating their ability to conduct a presentation (their role's argument) and participate in a debate. They are also assessed on how well they integrate the specific objective and learning outcomes.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /r/, /s/, and -ed endings).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of target vocabulary related to *robots* and *space exploration*.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of presented arguments and responses.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and respectful collaboration in group discussions and debates.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to questions and clarify details effectively.</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "Name one new idea about *robots* or *space exploration* that you learned or discussed today."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "Which specific grammar point (future perfect, passive voice, or zero conditional) did you use most effectively today, and how?"</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Review of lesson effectiveness)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. "How comfortable did you feel participating in the debate today? What was challenging about discussing abstract concepts?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. "What is one specific improvement you can make in your spoken English when talking about complex topics like *robotics* or *space*?"</li>
        </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The debate activity fostered engaging and active speaking practice, allowing students to genuinely use the target grammar and vocabulary in context. The role-play aspect helped facilitate critical thinking and respectful dialogue. Some students struggled with the nuances of the future perfect tense when formulating spontaneous arguments, and pronunciation of '-ed' endings will require further dedicated practice. For the next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for complex sentence construction during the writing phase and ensure more explicit checks for accurate pronunciation in individual interactions. I will also incorporate clearer roles for 'demonstrating a skill' to ensure that learning outcome is fully met by all students.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will synthesize the ideas and arguments from today's debate into a structured essay or report. They will use the target vocabulary and grammar to express their own informed opinions and solutions regarding *robots* and *space exploration* in a written format, further reinforcing the theme and linguistic content.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 minutes]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>